WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.340
- Larabee, that man who just entered.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.540
Do you know who that is?

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.770
- [Larabee] Which one, Chief?

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:05.820
- The man in the hat.

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.584
(dramatic music)

00:00:09.584 --> 00:00:10.650
(dreamy music)

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:11.880
(chimes ring)
(dreamy music)

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:13.590
That's Simon the Likable.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.410
- I don't care what his
name is, Chief. I like him.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.300
- Everybody at some point in their life

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.086
has met someone who they would
consider to be truly likable.

00:00:23.086 --> 00:00:23.961
(dreamy music)

00:00:23.961 --> 00:00:26.130
(chimes ring)
(dreamy music)

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:31.020
Like in the upper 99.9th
percentile of likability.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.820
These are the types of people who,

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.790
when you talk to them,
you feel understood.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.530
You feel like they're really listening,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.600
they value you as a human being,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.250
and they see the best in you.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.200
No matter where these types of people go,

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.300
it seems like they have friends.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.180
It seems like people
tend to celebrate them

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.480
and elevate them to this deity status.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.720
And it sounds kind of crazy,
but that's why it's so rare.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.930
I feel like I can only name
like three or four people

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.780
I have ever met who fit that description.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.990
And it seems like the more
likable of a person you are,

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.780
the more the world opens up to you.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.240
You know, people just
enjoy being around you,

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.250
they always want to invite you to things,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.080
they want you part of their company,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.550
they give you promotions.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:16.147
And you can look at that and say,

00:01:16.147 --> 00:01:18.240
"Well, that's not fair,
they don't deserve it.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.340
They're not more qualified than I am."

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.860
But it doesn't make it any less true.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.530
The more likable of a person you are,

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.050
the more people like you. (laughs)

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.870
You know, some people are very likable

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.720
and other people are
very difficult to like.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.340
And the people who are likable

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.580
tend to do better in
almost every area of life

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.090
compared to people that
no one wants to be around.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.120
I've thought a lot about
what makes somebody likable.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.900
What makes someone somebody
that people love being around?

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.640
Like, what's the common theme?

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.380
And can we implement those
behaviors into our own lives

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.230
so that we're just generally
more likable as a person?

00:01:58.230 --> 00:02:00.420
Now, right off the bat you
might have some resistance

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.427
towards this proposition because
you might be thinking like,

00:02:03.427 --> 00:02:05.730
"Oh, why would you want
to be more likable?

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:07.050
Why would you worry so much

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.370
about what other people think of you?

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.110
You're focusing on the wrong thing.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.780
You should focus on
yourself, your own happiness,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.090
and then people who don't
like it can screw off."

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:18.720
But I think that's overly
simplistic and foolish,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.070
and if you think that way

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.990
you're not gonna get very far in life,

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.480
especially if you already
struggle with people

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.910
and you think that, oh,
just being more of yourself

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.560
is gonna help for some reason.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.813
It's like, how has that
worked out for you?

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.363
How has that worked out for you?

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.340
Because ultimately,
we're creatures of habit.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:41.370
What we think is just
part of our personality

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.880
might just be a conversational
tic or bad habit

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.760
that we've picked up over conditioning,

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.140
over the role that we played

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.150
in our friends circle or our family,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.800
and it could actually be making us

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.100
more abrasive of a person than necessary.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.160
For instance, if you struggle
to make good eye contact

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.020
with somebody while
you're talking to them,

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.970
and it's just a habit that you have,

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.070
you tend to look down all the time,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.270
if I told you that

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.160
if you made better eye contact with people

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.360
and you got more comfortable

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.310
with making eye contact with people,

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.050
you're not gonna say that that is somehow

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.420
a betrayal of your personality.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:16.770
You wouldn't say that I'm telling you

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.510
to be someone that you're not.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.000
I'm not telling you to deny your god.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.700
I'm just saying that there
are things that you do

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:27.450
that make a difference in how
you are perceived by others.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.510
And I believe that the most likable people

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.470
just do those things better
than the average person.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.480
So in this video, it's a little
bit of a different video,

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.070
I'm just gonna talk off the cuff

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.960
about things that I've noticed

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.570
about the most likable
people I've ever met,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.000
and then hopefully we
can try to figure out

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:49.020
how to implement some of those
behaviors and those mindsets

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.230
to make us more likable and
more enjoyable to be around

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.420
so that we can enrich our lives

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:56.730
and have a better time with being social

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:58.170
and interacting with people,

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.265
leaving people better off
than when we found them.

00:04:00.265 --> 00:04:02.460
(dreamy music)

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.050
So the first thing that you need to do

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.720
if you want to increase your likability

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.100
is that you have to realize

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:11.670
that nobody really
cares about what you say

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:14.430
more than they care about
how you make them feel.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.760
How you make people feel
when you're around them

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.680
is the most important thing.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.050
That's the thing that leaves
an impression on people.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.180
In fact, 99 times out of 100,

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.240
people aren't gonna really
remember the things that you said

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.270
when they go home after
you interacted with them.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.820
They're gonna remember
how you made them feel.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.850
- [Narrator] Good manners
make good first impressions.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:39.403
They have a lot to do with
how well people like you.

00:04:39.403 --> 00:04:40.680
- A good example of this

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.840
is when somebody that you're talking to

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.690
is going on a rant about
all of their problems

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:47.580
and they're really heated and pissed off

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.650
and they're going on a monologue

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.120
about all the terrible things

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.160
that have happened to
them throughout the day.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.030
And you're listening

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.740
and you're kind of giving
them the time of day,

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.380
and then you try to
offer a piece of advice

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.750
or an observation or ask them a question,

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.730
but they just sort of steamroll over you

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:04.920
and disregard anything that you say

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.320
to continue in their monologue.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.810
When that happens, you
kind of feel dejected.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:11.040
You kind of stop listening

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.320
to all the particulars
of what they're saying,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.810
and the only thing that you're
left with is this feeling

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.527
that they don't really care
about what you have to say.

00:05:17.527 --> 00:05:19.800
You know, they don't really
care about your opinion,

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.030
they don't care about you,

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.653
so F this person.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.050
Every interaction that you have
with somebody is like that.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.040
What people remember about
you is how you made them feel.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.810
It's the vibe that you give off.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:36.960
Is it one that is
encouraging and empowering

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.780
and collaborative, reciprocal?

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:41.790
Or do you feel kind of expendable?

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.670
Like they don't really care
about your unique situation,

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.380
your unique perspective.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.910
They don't really care about any advice

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:48.870
that you have to give.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:51.180
They might as well be
ranting at a brick wall.

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.500
Here's another example.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:54.930
Say you're a guy and you're
on a date with some girl

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.240
and you are talking about all these things

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.680
that you think are very impressive.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.660
You're saying a lot of words.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.400
You're talking about
what you do for a living.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.997
You might be saying
stuff impressive, like,

00:06:03.997 --> 00:06:06.960
"Oh, I have a six-figure
income, blabbity-blabbity-blah.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.120
I have three sisters,
something-something-something."

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:10.200
And then once you realize

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:12.210
that you've been monologuing
for a little bit too long,

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:14.197
you ask routine questions to them like,

00:06:14.197 --> 00:06:15.960
"Oh, how many sisters do you have?

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.060
What's your favorite color,
blabbity-blabbity-blah?"

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.940
You know, this goes back and
forth for an hour or two,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:22.320
and she goes home.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.550
And while she's at home,

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:24.930
she was thinking back on the date

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:25.987
and she was thinking to herself,

00:06:25.987 --> 00:06:27.360
"You know, he seems like a nice guy,

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.700
but I just didn't feel that spark."

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.220
Like, "I didn't really feel
like we had chemistry."

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.950
That spark is everything.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:38.160
That spark is what people think of you.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.040
They don't think about all
the little things you said.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.040
They don't really think about
all the things that you did

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.750
or what your job position is.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.490
Like. No one really cares.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.560
What they care about is that spark,

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.560
and that spark is the energy you give off.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.530
How do you make that person
feel when they're around you?

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.050
If you're sitting there in
an interaction with somebody

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.260
and you are tense and
you are performative,

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:04.080
then they're going to be tense
and performative as well.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:07.530
The most likable people on
planet Earth realize this.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:10.890
You know, they put people at
ease because they're at ease.

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:12.990
- [Narrator] That's what good manners do.

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:15.600
They make everyone feel at ease.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.270
- They feel so comfortable
with conversation,

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.180
with eye contact, with being playful.

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.520
They realize that people like you

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.060
when you make them feel
important and valued.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:28.800
- [Narrator] That's what a boy likes.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.990
He wants to know he's appreciated.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.437
- You might hear that and say,

00:07:32.437 --> 00:07:34.020
"Okay, what am I supposed to do then?

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.660
Am I supposed to just be a pushover

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.000
and just never voice my opinion on things

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.400
and make people feel so
special and comfortable

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.740
and listen to all of their problems

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.410
and never talk about
my problems or my life,

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:49.050
and just be like an interviewer?"

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:50.190
That's not what I'm saying,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.320
and that's never the way it works out,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.120
because 9 times out of 10,

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:57.420
when you are genuinely
interested in someone else

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.150
and you act genuinely
interested in someone else,

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:03.150
they feel special and they
end up liking you more.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:04.830
And when someone likes you,

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:07.350
they're more likely to ask
you questions about yourself.

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:10.920
So it's almost like you have
to give first and then receive.

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.217
So another thing you might say is,

00:08:12.217 --> 00:08:14.070
"What, I'm just supposed to pretend

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:16.170
to be interested in what they're saying?

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:17.760
Like, that's not very genuine.

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:19.500
I thought that good connections

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:22.200
came from authenticity and honesty,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.290
so if I'm not honestly interested
in what they have to say,

00:08:25.290 --> 00:08:27.630
then the entire friendship
is built on a facade."

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:29.190
But here's a weird
thing that I've noticed,

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:30.870
is it's almost paradoxical

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.180
because the people who
are most comfortable

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.880
with who they are and
the life that they live

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:38.760
are usually the people
who are least desperate

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:40.320
to talk about themselves.

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.153
Because think about it.

00:08:41.153 --> 00:08:44.250
If you're insecure and
you don't like your life,

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.680
then everything that you say

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.170
about your life or your job position

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:52.560
or what you tend to do on Saturdays,

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.057
you're subconsciously hoping
that the other person says,

00:08:55.057 --> 00:08:57.960
"Oh, that's really cool. Oh, wow."

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.247
Like you want so desperately
for someone to say,

00:09:00.247 --> 00:09:01.980
"Wow, you make six figures.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.530
Oh, that's such an interesting
job. That's amazing."

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:05.370
But think about it.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:06.990
Everyone thinks like you.

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:08.550
Most people are insecure,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.680
so if you are that for people,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:11.887
if you're the person who says,

00:09:11.887 --> 00:09:14.940
"Oh, wow, that's super
interesting, tell me about that,"

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:16.680
people say, "Oh my goodness!

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.910
Let me tell you about it.

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:19.560
This is awesome. Someone's listening."

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.200
People are dying to be listened to.

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.290
People are dying to be
called by their name,

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:27.667
to be looked at in the eye, to feel like,

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:30.420
"Man, I don't know if that
guy really cares about me,

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:32.490
but I sure felt like he did

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.130
and that makes me feel good about myself."

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.450
That's the key to likability.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.880
So I think it all boils
down to a few things.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:42.390
If you want to be the most
likable person that you can be,

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:44.910
the most likable version of yourself,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:48.120
then firstly, you have to
live a life that you like.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:49.560
- [Narrator] The more you're interested

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:51.660
in the world around you,

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.580
the more interesting you become.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:55.890
- You have to find yourself likable.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.540
You have to admire the things

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.550
that you do on a day-to-day basis,

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.610
or at least not actively
participate in something

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:04.410
that ruins your opinion of yourself.

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.780
Because if you dislike yourself,

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:10.650
then curiosity in other
people is hard to come by.

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.440
You can't afford to be
curious about other people

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.510
because you need their validation.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.490
You need them to validate you.

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:19.350
But people who are secure in themselves

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.810
and their lifestyle will find that, oh,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.540
they don't necessarily need
people to comment on their life

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.130
because they like their life,

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.860
so then they can actually
have fun with conversation

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.960
and ask questions that
they find interesting

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.570
and be interested in the other person.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:10:35.580
And as a result of that,

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:37.260
the people that you're listening to

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:38.970
will say, "Wow I like this person.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.100
I want to ask them
questions about their life,"

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.010
and then that's when that
reciprocity can get going.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:45.720
Those are just some
thoughts that I've had.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.660
The people that I think are
the most likable in life

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:50.820
